 The teacher had 7 problems per worksheet. There are 9 remaining worksheets to grade. 
The key phrase in this problem is "how many more problems does she have to grade?"  We can approach this problem as if we were figuring out the "total" number of problems. The total number of problems she already graded would be the number of sheets already graded times the number of problems per sheet which is (7)(8) = 56 problems. The number of problems remaining is the number of sheets remaining times the number problems per sheet which is (7)(9) = 63 problems.  Then, we subtract the number of home problems from the number of remaining problems to find out how many more problems need to be graded. 63 - 56 = 7 answers
